---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const stages = [
  {
    name: 'Brief',
    body: 'We listen first — your goals, your users, your constraints, your budget and timeline. This stage ends with a written brief we both sign off on before any design starts.'
  },
  {
    name: 'Concept',
    body: 'Early spatial ideas and options, tested against the brief. You approve a single direction before we take it further.'
  },
  {
    name: 'Design Development',
    body: 'The concept resolved into real materials, finishes, systems and costs — the point where a design becomes something that can actually be built, and priced.'
  },
  {
    name: 'Construction Documents',
    body: 'Detailed drawings and specifications, sufficient for accurate pricing and for permitting.'
  },
  {
    name: 'Construction / Fit-Out',
    body: 'We build it — either directly, or as project manager coordinating contractors you already trust. Either way, the same team that designed it stays on site through delivery.'
  },
  {
    name: 'Handover',
    body: 'Snagging, as-built documentation, and a walkthrough before you move in.'
  }
];
---

<Layout
  lang="en"
  title="Process"
  description="How Omay Partners takes a project from brief to handover — the stages, who holds the contract, and what we need from you."
  translations={{ en: '/how-we-deliver/', tr: '/tr/nasil-teslim-ediyoruz/' }}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[62ch] px-[clamp(24px,5vw,96px)] py-16 md:py-16 lg:py-[104px]">
    <h1 class="text-h1">Process</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lead text-ink-60">
      "One team, from first sketch to handover" is the core of how we work — this page explains what that
      actually means in practice: the stages a project moves through, who is accountable for what, and why
      keeping design and delivery under one roof changes the outcome.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-h2">The stages</h2>
    <ol class="mt-6 space-y-8">
      {stages.map((stage, i) => (
        <li class="flex gap-5">
          <span class="font-sans-nums shrink-0 text-table text-ink-30">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-h3">{stage.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-body text-ink-60">{stage.body}</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-h2">Who holds the contract</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-ink-60">
      Depending on the project, we hold either a single design-and-build contract, or act as project manager
      coordinating contractors you already trust. Either way, the same team is accountable from the first
      sketch through handover — there is no separate design firm to hand off to, and no separate contractor to
      hand off from.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-h2">Why one team matters</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-ink-60">
      When design and construction sit with two separate firms, a gap opens between them — the architect
      points at the contractor for poor execution, the contractor points at the architect for impractical
      drawings, and the client is left in the middle resolving a dispute that isn't theirs to resolve. Keeping
      one team responsible for both closes that gap: the people who drew it are the same people responsible
      for building it, so problems get solved on site, not argued over in correspondence.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-14 text-h2">What we need from you</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-body text-ink-60">
      <li>A clear brief — goals, users, and constraints, as early and honestly as possible.</li>
      <li>Timely decisions at each stage, especially on materials and approvals — delays here delay everything after.</li>
      <li>Site access, and a realistic view of the site's condition and constraints.</li>
      <li>A realistic budget range up front, so the design we develop is one you can actually build.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>
